WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.997
Think of someone on your team who used to light

00:00:02.997 --> 00:00:05.994
up when they solved a hard case. Lately they arrive

00:00:05.994 --> 00:00:08.991
early, leave late and answer questions in a few clipped

00:00:08.991 --> 00:00:11.988
words. It is tempting to think they need to take

00:00:11.988 --> 00:00:14.985
better care of themselves. The research points somewhere else. The

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:17.982
World Health Organization describes burnout as the result of chronic

00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:20.080
workplace stress that has not been managed.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:24.270
It has three parts: exhaustion, mental distance from the job,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.110
and a sense of not making a difference. It is

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.950
a work phenomenon, not a medical condition. That tells you

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.790
where to look first. At the work. Researchers Christina Maslach

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.630
and Michael Leiter describe six areas where people and their

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.470
work can be mismatched. Workload. Control. Reward. Community. Fairness. Values.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.660
When the mismatch grows in any of these, burnout becomes

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.500
more likely. Meta-analyses tell a similar story. Job demands such

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.340
as conflicting instructions or constant interruptions raise burnout. Job resources

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.180
such as support and clear authority lower it. So what

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.020
helps? In a review of 18 controlled studies with physicians,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.860
changes to how work was organized, mostly reducing workload or

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.700
changing schedules, reduced burnout more than programs aimed at individuals.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.450
And a survey of more than 46,000 workers in the

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.850
United Kingdom found that people who joined resilience training, mindfulness

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.250
sessions or wellbeing apps appeared no better off than those

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.650
who did not. That survey cannot prove cause. But together

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.050
these findings point one way. Change the work first. Then

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.450
offer individual support as an addition. In human services there

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.810
is a second strain.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.632
Secondary traumatic stress comes from hearing about or helping with

00:01:36.632 --> 00:01:40.103
other people's trauma. It can look like intrusive thoughts, avoidance

00:01:40.103 --> 00:01:43.574
or feeling on edge. In one survey of 992 child

00:01:43.574 --> 00:01:47.046
welfare workers and supervisors, more than half reported such symptoms

00:01:47.046 --> 00:01:50.517
in the past week. It is related to burnout, but

00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:52.600
cutting paperwork will not touch it.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.448
What helps is attention to how trauma exposure is spread

00:01:56.448 --> 00:01:59.947
across the team, and support from colleagues and supervisors. The

00:01:59.947 --> 00:02:03.445
evidence on specific prevention programs is weak. So offer support,

00:02:03.445 --> 00:02:06.943
and let people choose how to use it. One last

00:02:06.943 --> 00:02:10.442
check. When you relieve one person, ask who picked up

00:02:10.442 --> 00:02:13.590
the work. Strain that moves has not been solved.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.192
Two weeks after any change, look again at hours, breaks

00:02:17.192 --> 00:02:20.443
and who is carrying the hardest calls. Prevention is not

00:02:20.443 --> 00:02:23.695
one fix. It is a habit of looking at the

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:24.020
work.
